------------------

there is some profoundly troubling code in here that clearly was
put there to optimize the code that whitesmith's was emitting:

declaring scalar types static when the compiler runs out of registers
will result in faster, smaller and very very broken code if the function
being called could have more than one process running in it due to sleeping
on a disk i/o.

these 2 processes will step on each other horribly.

ANY static declarations in any functions are very suspect, and either
need a lock, with all the deadlock possiblities that implies, or we
need to just KNOW that it, and any functions it calls, cannot ever sleep.


tracking this down is simply a matter of searching for the string 'static'.

--------------------

The u struct lives in its own 4K segment (USERSEG), remapped per
process, and the rest of that page is padding.  Pack that padding with
pure-text leaf functions - the ccc runtime (csv / q* / ldiv / tramp) and
whatever other dataless helpers fit - so the page is not wasted, and
move as much of it in there as possible.

Identify and move constant data onto the page too: the static strings
(the devname / panic messages), the syscall dispatch table (syssw), the
block and char switch tables (biosw / ciosw), the specs / mws drive
tables.  They are identical across processes and read but never written,
so they belong on the filled-out page and fork skips copying them the
same way.  Audit each one for writes first - anything ever written has
to stay in ordinary data.

Then on fork, rather than segcopy-ing the whole page (the u struct plus
the shared leaf code) into a fresh segment each time, keep a freelist
of "filled out" pages that already carry the leaf code.  fork takes one
of those, copies only the ~629 byte u struct into it, and skips the text
copy; exit returns the page to the list instead of freeing it.  The leaf
code is identical across every process, so this saves both the copy and
the re-fill on every fork.

Open: where the list lives, how a fresh page is primed the first time
(one segcopy of the leaf code), and how a swapped-out process interacts
with it.

The u page is laid out by the linker as three objects in one segment
(USERSEG, sys.h): the leaf code first at the base, then ustack (user.c)
- the system stack - and then the u struct itself, whose register save
area comes first in the struct because that is where the firmware's
trap entry leaves SP.  So the kernel's C stack grows down from
&u.stack into ustack.

ustack and u are .bss, and the file carries no bytes for either.  That
is what let the page move up to segment 15: the kernel and the loader
never run at the same time, so the loader's stack lands on the u
struct's addresses during the load, and the page only has to keep its
*code* below the loader.  task0_body() zeroes u before it is used,
since what the loader leaves there is its own stack.

--------------------

Three window pages used to have to exist at once: 0xd000 for the
superblock, 0xe000 for the u page, 0xf000 for the buffer data window.
The same three pages are in play, but the u page has moved up into 15
(0xf000) and the buffer window took its place at 0xe000, so what is
held now is 0xe000 by the window and 0xd000 only for the length of a
user-memory copy (mem.s borrows and restores it).  It matters because
the buffer pool's headers are minted contiguously off &blist[8] ==
_ebss, so a page held by anything else hides whatever headers land in
it - 0xd000's page was dead space for exactly that reason.

The superblock used to hold 0xd000 across calls and sleeps, which meant
that page could not carry headers - the pool stopped at 0xd000.  It is
plain memory now: getsb() (uio.c) copies the block into a per-device
sbslot in .bss once and points the buffer's data/xmem at the slot, so
sb->data stays an ordinary pointer, nothing has to be re-pinned after a
sleep, and the driver still writes the right bytes because its DMA
address is (xmem << 12) | (data & 0xfff), not the window address.  mem.s
restores the page it borrows for user memory from IMAGE0+26, so with the
superblock out of the window system nothing holds 0xd000 and the headers
carry on through it.  bzero() and bget()'s reuse path call sbrel() to
give a recycled buffer its window mapping back.

The cost is the slots: NMOUNT * (512 + 6) = 2072 bytes of .bss, about 98
headers, so the pool gains ~90 rather than the ~195 the page is worth.
The two leftovers are still available if the pool ever needs to be
bigger again:

  - Put the superblock back in a segment and borrow window 13 for each
    access (save the mapping, map, copy, restore), with bcopy() moved
    into the u page so only u-page code runs while the borrow is held.
    That gives up the ~2072 bytes of slots and frees the page for
    headers - the full ~195 - but requires a discipline the slot design
    does not: no header access (bget/brelse/bdwrite) between mapping the
    window and restoring it.  A miss there is silent filesystem
    corruption, which is why the slots went in first.

  - The u page is in segment 15 now, so the pool's ceiling is one page
    away (0xf000 instead of the window's 0xe000) and it roughly
    doubles.  It needs the buffer data window to become borrowed at
    every site that uses it: bwin() holds its segment for the whole of
    a buffer access, so headers parked in 0xe000's page would be read
    back as buffer data.  Borrowing means save the map register, map,
    copy, restore - and no header access (bget/brelse/bdwrite) while a
    borrow is held, the same discipline the superblock slots avoided.
    expand_bufs()'s bound is BUFWIN (sys.h) today; this is the change
    that moves it up to _upage.

The boot loader's own stack sits at the very top of memory (mwboot1.s
parks sp at 0xffff and the second level inherits it), and the loader
keeps using it for the whole kernel load.  Measured in d1 by sampling sp
per instruction over the whole trap-free pre-kernel window (cycle 462452
to 709169), the loader's deepest sp is 0xffde - thirty-three bytes below
the top, so the loader's stack is not what caps the image.  What caps it
is the block rounding: boot.c's load() writes whole 512 byte blocks from
0x0ff0, so the last block starts at 0xfdf0, and the 128-block list
reaches past the top of memory before it runs out - the practical ceiling
is 120 blocks.

The kernel is linked so the file's data stream stops short of the
loader's stack: 0x1000 + text + data, which today is 0xf000 + the leaf
code's size, ends at 0xfcc9 - 119 blocks, 789 bytes clear of the
loader's deepest sp.  What actually binds the leaf code now is the u
page itself, not the loader: leaf + ustack (512) + u (117) has to fit
in 4096, so the leaf code has 0xf3e bytes and is using 0xcc9, leaving
194 bytes to grow into.

The same sampling gives the kernel's own stack: the deepest supervisor
sp is 0xede5 at adiv+21, 228 bytes of the 512 byte ustack, so the
number in user.c is generous and is the one to turn if the page ever
has to get smaller.

--------------------

V7's big exec change over V6 was the environment: exec(2) takes a third
argument, an array of "NAME=value" strings (envp), that it copies onto the
child's stack next to argv.  Micronix has none of it - no getenv/putenv in
libc, no `environ` in crt0, no shell variables to export.

It is not a big lift: envp is just another pointer array laid on the stack
with a string payload, exactly like argv, and exec already builds that
stack in putargs().  The pieces are:

  - exec(2) / execve: a third parameter (envp), stacked after argv, plus
    an `environ` pointer (u.environ) and argc/argv/envp aligned the way
    crt0 expects.
  - crt0: set up `environ` from the stack and pass it to main.
  - libc: getenv(3) / putenv(3) (or setenv), and `extern char **environ`.
  - the shell: export/name=value syntax and passing its environment to
    each exec.

--------------------

mw.c stops at 8 heads, and 16 is not a table entry away.  The head
select field in the command structure is three bits:

	hedsel = pcmp<<7 | hicur<<6 | (~head&7)<<2 | drv

so the 8th head is the last one that fits.  The two bits above the head
field are per-track flags the driver sets out of the table, and they are
not the same kind of thing:

	bit 6, hicur - the write current control.  mw.c sets HIGHCUR 0x40
	there and clears it at track >= lowcur, so the driver drops to low
	current partway out.

	bit 7, pcmp - write precompensation.  This is a different function
	and not a current setting at all: it retimes the write data on the
	inner tracks to compensate for bit shift, so it changes the data
	clocking.  It stays.

On the HD-DMA cable bit 6's wire is the same wire as the fourth head
select line, so a 16-head drive overloads it - and only it.

Nothing worth having is lost by giving the pin to head select.  Low
current on the larger MFM drives is generated by the drive itself - it
knows what cylinder it is on and modulates its own write current - and
the line exists for the early drives that had to be told.  Even smaller
later 8-head drives routinely ignore it.  So the wire was on its way
out anyway, and the drives large enough to want 16 heads are the ones
that stopped needing to be told.

That makes specs[]'s lowcur, and the HIGHCUR half of hedsel, things to
remove rather than work around, and it is the removal that has to come
first: hedsel widens to (~head&15)<<2, bit 6 goes with it, and the
precompensation at bit 7 does not move.  Any drive that does still want
its current commanded needs the decision made before the field widens,
not after.

None of the types are in the way - struct info.heads is a UINT8 and 16
fits - and neither is specs[]: it is a table, and it loses a column
rather than gaining a row.

--------------------


--------------------

sys/ncr.c is an NCR 5380 SCSI host adapter.  It did not fit the resident
kernel, and it is not in it now either: it is the fourth overlay module
(major 5), placed from its appended page at boot like the other three.
It was sys/scsi.c until the module split - the file and the module are
named for the card, the device nodes stay named for the bus (scsi*).

The driver is 3241 bytes (text 3034, data 146, bss 61).  The resident
kernel had 3086 bytes to spare, which was 155 short; dj.c's 1K staging
buffer leaving the 64K bought 639, so the same driver is 484 to spare.
That is a change of state rather than a margin, and it is worth saying
that the reclaim is the whole of it - see OVERLAY-DRIVERS.md, where the
same 639 bytes are already spoken for.  The budget, measured on the
linked image (mxnm unix, and mxnm -b on the folds; the same numbers are
the first sixteen bytes of unix, a Whitesmiths header - text 0x8e73,
data 0x5e6b, bss 0x275, text_base 0x1000, data_base 0x9e73):

	text + data + bss ends at _ebss = 0xc8d9
	-Shigh parks highmem.o (2202 bytes) AFTER bss, and win.o is
	pinned at 0xe000, so the link wants _ebss <= 0xe000-2202 = 0xd766
	0xd766 - 0xc8d9 = 3725 bytes of headroom
	3725 - 3241 = 484 bytes to spare

That is the link's budget.  The pool is a second one, and it is the one
that binds, because MAXBUFS (256, main.c) caps the count before BUFWIN
caps the memory: blist's 8 boot headers at 0xc831 plus 248 minted off
_ebss end at 0xdd31, 719 bytes under 0xe000.  So the pool is not full
yet - the first 719 bytes of kernel growth are free, and every 21 after
that costs one buffer.  A driver that used the whole 3725 would leave
about 110.  scsi.o is the case to reckon with: at 3241 it spends the 719
free bytes and then 121 buffers, so it arrives with a pool of 135
instead of 256.  Fitting the link and keeping the cache are not the same
question, and this is where that shows.  The resident kernel's space is
bounded by memory; its cache is bounded by MAXBUFS.

Room for this was bought once already, by moving code rather than
deleting it: mw.c's and ide.c's separate label decodes became
sys/dlabel.c and the two 512-byte labelbufs went away, which is
text -580, data -1024, bss +66 and _ebss down 1538.  scsi.c has since
been moved onto the same decode - it had the third copy of it, and its
own 512-byte labelbuf - and that is text -602, data -394, bss -4, so
scsi.o went 4241 -> 3241 and the shortfall 1155 -> 155, which dj.c's
reclaim then closed.  The same lever is what option 2 below means, and
it is the one that has worked.

KMEM.md's layout map carries these numbers too - it is the place to read
the shape rather than recompute it, and it went stale by about 3.8K once
before this.  The link fails with "objects out of address order (data)"
the moment _ebss+2202 crosses 0xe000.

There is no page to move it to either: win.o is 3915 of 4096 and
upage.o is 2837 + 629 of 4096.

What is wired now.  sys/ncr.c is the driver; sys/ncrhdr.c is the module
header, a separate object because an object's data is placed in the
order the objects are named, so the header is alone in the one named
first - major 5, init &ncrinit, bvec &ncrbvec, line 2, intr &ncrint,
name ncr5380.  sys/ncrinit.c is the probe, and it lives in highmem.o
with the other module inits rather than in the driver's page: a probe
that runs once at boot does not belong in the 4K it is testing for, and
highmem is parked past bss and reclaimed as buffer headers, so it costs
the running kernel nothing.  GNUmakefile has ncr in MODS and ncrinit.o
in HIGHMEM, consts.c has the sixth devname[] slot and the sixth biosw[]
row - which is what takes nbdev to 6, ovlplace refusing a major at or
past both nbdev and ncdev - and dev/devlist has the nodes.  The target
is the low three bits of the minor, so scsi0..scsi7 are b 5 0..7 and the
whole-disk slices scsi0c..scsi7c are b 5 64..71.

The probe reads the initiator command register, not the mode register.
A port with no card behind it reads 0xff, but so does CSBS: current SCSI
bus status is active-low bus lines, and an idle bus reads all ones, so a
bare port read cannot tell a card at rest from an empty socket.  ICR is
the cpu's own latch - the driver writes 0 and reads back, and the six
bits it can write (RST, ACK, BSY, SEL, ATN, DATA) come back clear on a
card that is there and set on a port nothing answers.  The mask and the
rest of the reasoning are in include/sys/ncr.h.

scsii2int() is gone.  It chained the 5380's handler to ideint() by hand,
which cannot survive the split - ideint is in another module and the
kernel has no address for it at link time - so the resident fan-out
does it instead: ovlntr (sys/ovl.c) holds two handlers per line and
calls each through ovlcall with its own page mapped.  That fan-out is
the one resident change the SCSI work needed, and it is what lets ide
and ncr both live on VI2.

Which way it went: sys/overlay.md's, the third of the three that were
listed here.  Option 1 was never attractive - it buys the link by giving
up a driver, and running all four is the point.  Option 2 had already
been spent: the reclaims that closed the 155-byte link shortfall
(dlabel.c, dj.c's staging buffer) are in the numbers above, and what is
left is the pool, which is a budget and not a reclaim.  So the driver
left the kernel instead, which is what the overlay was written for.

Verified, negative first because it is the cheap one: with no card and
no volume the page is not placed (ovl: 5 not placed in the boot log),
major 5 answers as nodev - ovlopen calls nodev, ovlstrat returns ENXIO
through iodone, ovlbvec[5] never having been filled - and the pool is
unchanged at 254 buffers.  A failed probe spends its page and nothing
else.

One thing about the driver itself is worth knowing.
A 5380 cannot interrupt on "a data byte is ready" - that is DRQ, the
DMA pin, and there is no DMA controller here - so the byte handshake is
polled by the cpu in the caller's context.  The interrupt covers the
bus going free (MONITOR BSY in the mode register) and parity faults,
which is what the chip has to offer an initiator with no DMA.  Sleeping
through the seek needs the target to disconnect and be reselected, and
this driver has no reselect.

------------------

Two drivers calling each other is a door the overlay discipline had no
answer for, and it cost a boot.

sys/bus.c is the DMA bus lock mw and dj share: a driver that wants the
bus for a transfer asks for it (busget) and gives it back (busgive), and
one that asks while another holds it is remembered as the heir and
called when the holder lets go.  The lock kept a bare pointer per
caller, and a pointer into a module is an offset from OVLBASE that means
nothing unless that module's page is the one mapped.  So: dj's djint
held the bus; mw's strategy routine called busget(&mwstart), found the
bus taken, and was registered as the heir; dj's busgive then did
(*bus)() with djdma's page still in the window.  The cpu ran mw's
offset inside djdma's page - 0x94e6, which in djdma is the middle of
copy()'s staging block - with djdma's data under it and djdma's bss
temporaries zeroed, and the LDIR that followed walked across the Z280's
on-chip register file at 0x0400, wrote the switch register, and the
emulator switched tasks mid-instruction.  The runaway sled that came out
the other end hung the shell at sync and at umount, and on another run
made a mount of /dev/fla report "not a file system" on an image that was
byte-identical to one that mounted cleanly.

It was found by reading the trace and not the source: -t all, then the
two hundred instructions before the hang, where LD HL,(0xfae7) is the
heart of busgive and 0xfae7 is bus.  The heir in next was 0x94e6, and
nm reports _mwstart: 04ca, which with nm's -0x100 and the module base
0x901c is 0x94e6.  mw.c:388 is busget(&mwstart) and nothing else
registers one.

The fix is the same discipline applied to the fourth door: bus.c stores
a segment beside each pointer, captures it from image0[2 * OVLSEG] where
the pointer is registered - which is the registrant's own segment,
because a module registers from inside itself - and calls the heir
through ovlcall (sys/ovl.c), which is exported for it and maps the page
for the call and restores the interrupted map after.  All seven call
sites (dj.c 648, 702, 812, 1522, 1531; mw.c 388, 410) are reached with
their own module's page mapped, and that is what makes the capture
sound.  Cost: 42 bytes, all of it in the u page - bus.o 125 -> 167,
upage.o 2837 + 629 of 4096 as it stood then (2862 + 630 now; the
paragraph below says what moved it).

Verified: all four filesystems (root on the m16 volume, mw unit 1 on
/dev/m16b, ide unit 0 on /dev/ide0, the floppy on /dev/fla) mounted at
once; a file written through each; each read back through another
driver's path; sums and cmp agreeing; sync; three umounts; and then the
host tooling - fsck, icheck and dcheck on the three volumes and on the
floppy with its delta merged - each clean, 0 bad.

What was open here, and what closed it.  A driver that is a module and
sleeps inside a strat could wake to another major's page, and it was not
hypothetical: idestart() (ide.c:626), scsistart() (ncr.c:745) and
busplease() (dj.c:1523) all sleep inside a module, waiting on a board or
on the bus.  The four-driver test reached it, and did not need a second
process to do it, because the bus lock is a door: busgive() (sys/bus.c)
calls its heir through ovlcall(), and dj's heir, busready() (dj.c:1522),
sleeps on the bus inside dj's page while running in the context of the
process that gave the bus up, so that process is parked in a module page
its own u.drv does not name.  The staging below is what came of it.

The fix is the process's own driver, in the process's own u page.
struct user gains drv (include/sys/proc.h), holding the segment of the
page the process is in, 0 for none.  ovlmap() records the segment it just
mapped, and ovlcall() records the callee's, putting the caller's back
when the call returns - so the door the bus lock uses is covered, and a
driver that returns from an heir and later sleeps in its own code is
still right.  ovlremap() (sys/ovl.c) writes that segment back as it
stands: what was recorded is the value the register wants, so there is no
major to look up and no ovlseg[] to consult.  Both doors write u.drv
before the map register, so a switch taken between the two writes puts
the page being entered back and not the one being left; ovlremap() itself
runs inside newmap()'s di().  newmap() calls it at every switch
(sys/malloc.c), which is the one place the map changes under a process -
and by then the u page is the incoming process's, so u.drv is that
process's page and not the one that was just running.  A process that is not inside a driver has 0 there and is
left alone; one that is inside a driver is put back in its own page
however many other drivers ran while it was out.  The window is still
one page for the machine.  What became per-process is the knowledge of
which page a process is owed.

That is also what makes the non-blocking corollary in overlay.md not
apply here.  A swapped-in frame's bytes would be another driver's only
in a design that swaps contents; this one maps a window onto a segment
each module holds for the whole boot, so restoring the window restores
the bytes.  An overlaid function here may sleep.

Cost: resident text a few bytes either way - the last text symbol,
_console, is at 0x854a, where the note for the revision this replaced
recorded 0x8550, because ovlremap() lost the ovlseg[] lookup and ovlmap()
stores a segment where it stored a major, which pays for ovlcall()'s two
stores - and no change at all in the u page, because drv stayed one byte:
struct user
117 -> 118 as before, upage.o 2862 + 630 of 4096, u ending at 0xfda4 with
604 bytes free.  _ebss is 0xcbc9, the same figure it had before the fix,
so both budgets that matter, the resident end and the pool, did not move.
Verified by booting a fresh create_vol'd volume: the placement lines, the
buffer count and the hdr range are unchanged (254 buffers, hdr range
cbc9..dfe2), and the boot runs on into single-user and /usr/src's make.

The race itself was then staged, and what this note first said about the
staging was wrong; corrected 2026-09-26 from the run logs, which are the
evidence for all of it.

Three kernels were built, each with its own create_vol'd volume: the fix
as it stood (the major in u.drv, ovlremap() looking it up in ovlseg());
the same with ovlremap()'s body emptied; and the fix with a probe in
ovlremap() that prints whenever the page it is about to write differs
from the one in place.  All three ran the same work - three filesystems
mounted (m16b, ide0, fla), a 207375-byte pipeline copy of the floppy's
lib/*.a onto the ide drive, large enough to force real strat calls
instead of sitting in the buffer cache, then sums, sync, three umounts,
and a host-side comparison of what landed on ide against those same files
read out of the floppy image.  Each of them completed, and the copies
were identical to the reference.  The probe fired forty times inside the
first minute of the copy, capped there, turning over between seg 17 (dj)
and seg 18 (ide): the precondition - a process switched in while the
window holds another driver's page - is reached constantly, as soon as
two drivers are in use at once.

What is false is the step from that to "so the consequence is out of
reach".  It is not: the same kernel text crashes it.  sys/unix as it
stood at 17:37:58 died inside that copy - r6, and again at 18:03 on
volumes built separately, rb - both times entering the ROM monitor with
the same 28-byte dump at 0xFCD4.  And that kernel is the same program as
the one that completed the copy at 16:53:10: the two images differ in six
bytes, five of them the Created stamp and the sixth the root device, 3/0 as
the link leaves it and 3/8 as the copy installed on a volume is stamped.
Which program ran is not in doubt beyond that - the Created line is the
kernel's own - and it is the volume's /micronix that executes: -S hands d1
a symbol file, nothing more, and does not write the kernel it names
(checked by mtime across a boot).  Text, modules and symbol table are
identical.  Same program, one completion, two crashes.

The completion was timing, then, and not safety.  hwsim/d1 keeps no clock
and sleeps nowhere, so the emulated CPU is deterministic, but the harness
feeds console lines on a wall-clock schedule, and the interleaving of the
copy's I/O with those lines is not reproducible run to run.  What that
interleaving sometimes buys is the wrong page: the process parked in
busready() is inside dj's page, its u.drv names the module it was in
before, and ovlremap() puts ovlseg[u.drv] back - the caller's driver - so
the heir resumes in another driver's bytes.  ovlcall() not recording
u.drv is the whole of it, and the fix above is that gap closed.

The kernel without the fix is no more of a control than the one with it:
it leaves the window as the last ovlcall() left it, and for the waking
path that is the callee's own page, so the same park usually comes back
to the right page by accident.  That is a race in the other direction,
and it is why three completions were not evidence.

They were not three completions, either, and reading the staging again is
where this note's last paragraph starts.  The runs are r1 to r6 and ra, rb;
their consoles are in /tmp/conc and say what the paragraph above does not.
r1 and r2 did complete, and their copies are identical by the guest's own
sum and cmp - but what they copied is libmdc.a, 11774 bytes, which fits in
the 254-buffer cache eleven times over and never reaches the strat path
that paragraph says the copy exists to force.  The 207375-byte copy was run
twice.  r3 - Created 17:09:49, the same text r2 ran - completed it:
big-done, three umounts, a remount, all-done.  r5 did not, and what it ran
is the probe build, Created 17:27:55, a text of its own: its console ends
with the harness's own lines echoed and nothing after them, no big-done and
no all-done, a shell that never got to run them, the probe's last line the
last thing the kernel printed.  It was read as a completion because r3
completed, and it was a stall - ten minutes before the crashes at 17:37:58,
and three quarters of an hour before the runs below.  r3 is the last
completion, and it is the rare outcome.

Every 207375-byte run since either crashes or stops.  Five of them left a
destination image behind, and the images say how far each got: ab 80 blocks
of the copy's 405, cc 100, cd 89, re1 117, rf1 118 - each measured by
diffing the image against the pristine template it was made from, and each
a contiguous run from the same first block, with the same all-zero block
eighth.  Their root volumes say which text ran: ab and cc Created 17:37:58,
cd 18:12:53, re1 and rf1 18:22:43 - three texts, five depths, and not one
of the depths shared by the two runs that shared a text.  So where it stops
is timing, like the crash, and not one place.  The two that were watched to
a stall sat in the same state and stayed there: everything asleep, the
source cat on _blist[8] (dev 0308
blk 758, flags BBUSY|BSYNC|BDELWRI|BWANT and no BDONE, which is the waiter
that makes the sleep permanent), the destination cat on the pipe inode
b6c4, sh in wait - while the kernel ran sched()'s `while (next == 0)` loop
at PC 0x516c on 100% of the emulated cpu, rchar and wchar frozen across
25-second samples, and no host disk I/O for minutes.  That buffer is the
only one of the 254 without BDONE, so this is not an avalanche of unfinished
work: it is one write that never answered.  It is not slowness either -
nothing in the machine is runnable.

That write is the pipe's, and the pipe's blocks are on the root device, so
what was lost is a root-disk completion; every ide write has answered.  The
dump cannot say which door it was lost in, and there are three: the root
driver's own path, which is the stamped one - ovlseg[3] is OVLSEG itself,
so putting the window back there is the identity map and a wrong record is
invisible; the bus lock's heir, called through ovlcall from whatever
process was holding the bus; and the fan-out over line 2, which is new.  A
-t all trace of the instructions before the stall separates them, and it
has not been run.  What the destination device does say is that the copy
was never a file: its inode did not reach the disk and neither did its
directory entry, so what is on ide is a run of blocks holding the copy's
first bytes in order, with nothing pointing at them, and one block inside
that run that is all zeros - whether a hole or a block no list mentions is
not tellable without the inode.

ide's own park is still not reached, and the reason for that part of the
old note stands: hwsim's IDE does the whole sector inside the port write
and raises its interrupt on the way out (d1/ide.c, ide_command), so
idewait()'s sleep is satisfied by an interrupt taken inside sched()'s
enable() and the process is never switched out holding an ide frame.  On
a real drive it is the ordinary case: the milliseconds idewait and
scsistart spend asleep there are the whole reason they sleep.  A test that
reaches that one needs the emulator faithful on this point - a delay
between the command write and the interrupt - which is written down as
work and not done.

One smaller thing that fix does not cover, and that nothing today
reaches.  wakeup() matches on the event address alone, which two modules
could in principle share because every module is linked at OVLBASE; the
events in use today do not collide (dj's djcomm, ide's and ncr's
per-board flags), but dj's two waits are the unguarded kind - a bare
sleep with no loop re-testing the condition - so a false match there
would resume a command that had not completed.

The other smaller thing this note used to list was the bug, and the note
had the facts backwards: "neither heir sleeps (dj's busready() wakes and
returns)".  busready() does sleep - dj.c:1522-1523, sleep(busready,
DJPRIORITY) when the bus is not free - and it is the heir that runs in
the caller's context, so it is the park the crash above is.  mw's
mwstart() is the heir that does not sleep; it returns when it cannot get
the bus.  ovlcall() records the segment now, so both heirs are covered.

The trace that last paragraph was waiting on has been run, and the door was
not one of the three: it was where the lock's own words live.  With the
buffer clamp on (DEBUGBUFS=1, 32 buffers) the same workload stalls sooner -
the destination image stops changing at block 20 of the copy's 405, where
the unclamped runs reached 80 to 148 - and a -t all trace of that run
carries every access to the four words busget and busgive keep.  They were
statics in bus.c, and bus.c is leaf code: it links into upage.o, so the
words were in the u page, and in that run the same four words were touched
at six different physical addresses, one apiece.  next, the heir, was
written exactly twice in the whole run, both times by the process
registering mwstart as the heir, and no read of it ever came back anything
but 0, so no busgive ever took its heir branch.  The end of the trace is
the mechanism in order: the heir registered in one process's copy, then a
busget and a busgive in another process - the one holding the bus - reading
its own next as 0 and clearing its own bus.  From there the remaining 60 MB
of trace holds no hddma command at all, and the root device's queue keeps
the pipe's buffer at BBUSY|BSYNC|BDELWRI|BWANT with no BDONE: mwstart was
never called, so the command it was to issue was never issued, and the
sleep waiting for that command had nothing left to wake it.

What makes it fatal is that a transfer's busget to its busgive is not a
pair inside one process.  mw's mwstop sleeps waiting for its controller
before it gives the bus up, and dj's busplease sleeps waiting for the bus,
woken by the heir call itself; either way another process runs between a
registration and the give, and a busgive run there reads its own copy of
the heir, finds 0, and drops the heir the sleeping process registered.  One
bus for the machine cannot live in a page that is one per process.  The
four words are in ovl.c now, beside the module tables and for the reason
those are there: kernel data, identity-mapped, one copy for the whole
machine.  What stays in the u page is the code, because a function two
modules both call has to be somewhere both can reach, and one module cannot
call into another; each pointer keeps its segment, because the heir is
usually in the other module and is entered through ovlcall.  The cost is
the four words themselves: _ebss 0xcbc9 to 0xcbd1, and the buffer headers
with it (hdr range cbd1..cdb4).

The fix is checked the same way, at the same clamp: the words are read and
written at one physical address for the whole run (mapped:00cb25, the
identity map), the heir branch runs - 549 reads of it that are not 0, both
ways round, mw handing the bus to dj 226 times and dj handing it to mw 323
- and the copy finishes, 405 blocks where the broken clamp managed 20.
ls -l says 207375 bytes and both sums read 24620 406: the copy's names the
file, the sources' names `-`, and the two are one file.  That is the whole
of this failure.  The other two doors the note listed were not in it - the
root driver's own path, which is the stamped one, and the line-2 fan-out
both stand as they were - and the same class of check was made against the
rest of the u page and came back clean: the leaf assembly has no writable
storage (one read-only month table) and consts.c's tables are written when
a module is placed, before the swapper forks, so every copy of them is the
same by design.  What those two cases draw, and what bus.c now says at the
point of the mistake, is the rule: the u page is for code two modules share
and for tables written before the fork, and anything written after that
belongs to the kernel's own data.
